# React State: Understanding the Mechanics

`useState` is the strangest thing in React so far. A plain function call, with no argument identifying which component or which variable, somehow remembers a value across renders. In the video, we built it into freact, and once you have read that code, the rules around hooks stop being arbitrary.

The updated project is in `freact/04-state/` next to this notebook. To watch it run, right-click `index.html` in that folder and choose "Show Preview", then click the button and watch the count go up. This reading walks through what changed since `freact/03-events/`: rendering got split in two, and a `useState` function appeared, backed by an array.

## Where the Value Actually Lives

Not in your component. Your component function is called fresh on every render; nothing inside it survives.

freact keeps **an array of hooks**, outside every component. Each `useState` call in a component owns one slot in that array. The slot holds the current value, and `useState` hands back that value plus a function for changing it.

Which slot? The one at the current position. freact keeps a counter, `hookIndex`, that starts at zero for each render and moves up by one on every hook call.

```{walkthrough}
title: The hooks machinery
step: The array and the counter
file: freact/04-state/freact.js
region: hooks
Two module-level variables are the entire storage system.
@"let hooks": Every state variable on the page lives in here, identified by nothing but its position.

step: useState
file: freact/04-state/freact.js
region: use-state
Read it in three parts: claim a slot and fill it on the first render, define a setter for later, and hand both back.
@"hooks[idx] === undefined": First render only. On every later render the slot is already occupied, so the initial value is ignored -- which you have seen: `useState(0)` does not reset your count on re-renders.
@"typeof initialVal === 'function'": React also accepts a function as the initial value, called once to compute it. freact supports the same.
@"typeof newVal === 'function'": The updater form, `setClickCount((c) => c + 1)`. The function is called with the value currently in the slot, so it reads the freshest value there is, not the one captured when the handler was created.
@"hooks[idx] !== newVal": The setter compares before it acts. Setting state to the value it already has changes nothing and schedules nothing -- true in freact and in React.
@"setTimeout(_performRender, 0)": The re-render happens on a later tick, so the rest of the handler finishes first. Keep this line in mind; it explains the most common React surprise, below.
@"hookIndex++": Claiming slots by call order. The first `useState` in a render reads slot 0, the second slot 1, and so on.
@"return [hooks[idx], setState]": A two-element array, which is why the call site destructures: `const [clickCount, setClickCount] = useState(0)`.
```

That position-is-identity design is the whole trick, and it is why the rules of hooks exist:

- **Hooks are called in the same order every render**, because the order is the identity. The first `useState` call gets slot 0 every time.
- **Hooks are not called inside conditionals or loops**, because skipping one call shifts every later hook into the wrong slot, and a component would silently read some other variable's value.

## Rendering Had to Split in Two

Until now, rendering happened once, when `main` called `render`. A state change has to re-render **without** anyone calling `render` again, so the video split the job.

```{walkthrough}
title: render, split in two
step: Remembering what and where
file: freact/04-state/freact.js
region: render-root
Two variables record the arguments `render` used to consume directly.
@"rootComponent": What to render and where to put it, saved so a state change can do the same job again later.

step: render just records and delegates
file: freact/04-state/freact.js
region: render
The public entry point now only stores the root component and container, then calls the function that does the work.
@"_performRender();": main code calls `render` once; freact itself calls `_performRender` again on every state change. The underscore is the same convention as `_createDomNode`: internal machinery.

step: _performRender does one full render
file: freact/04-state/freact.js
region: perform-render
Reset the counter, wipe the container, rebuild the DOM from the stored description.
@"hookIndex = 0": Every render starts at slot 0. Delete this line and the second render would start claiming slots where the first left off -- every state variable would silently read some other slot. This reset is what keeps "same order, same slot" true.

step: One character in the text branch
file: freact/04-state/freact.js
region: text-node
The video ends with a tiny bug fix on this line: it used to read `nodeValue || ''`.
@"nodeValue ?? ''": A count of `0` is falsy, so `||` replaced it with an empty string and the button read "You clicked  time(s)". The **nullish coalescing operator** `??` only substitutes for `null` and `undefined`, so `0` renders as `0`.
```

## The Component

```{walkthrough}
title: A click counter on freact
file: freact/04-state/App.jsx
The component from the video, and it is exactly the shape you write in real React.
@"useState(0)": Runs during every render, but only the first one stores the `0`. Each render's `clickCount` is whatever is in slot 0 at that moment.
@"setClickCount(clickCount + 1)": The handler calls the setter from *its* render. The setter writes the slot and schedules `_performRender`; the next render calls `App` again and gets the new value back from `useState`.
@"{clickCount}": This lands in the description as a text child, which is why the `??` fix mattered: on the very first render its value is the falsy `0`.
```

Preview it and click. Every click is: setter writes the array, a re-render is scheduled, `App` runs again, `useState` returns the new value, and the whole DOM is rebuilt with the new text. Nothing about the component "remembers" anything; the array outside it does.

## Why the Old Value Lingers

The `setTimeout` line has a visible consequence: the state variable **still holds the old value** on the line after you call the setter, because the re-render has not happened yet. This is the single most common surprise in React, so see it in the real thing:

```{react autorun}
function Surprising() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

  function handleClick() {
    setCount(count + 1);

    console.log('right after setCount, count is still ' + count);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Count is {count}</button>;
}

renderReact(<Surprising />);
```

Click it and read the log. `count` in that handler is the value from the render that created the handler. It does not change; a *new* render produces a new `count`. When the next value depends on the current one, the updater form `setCount((c) => c + 1)` is the reliable way to say so -- you read the line in `useState` that makes it work.

## Exercises

1. Complete `useState` so it claims a slot, stores the initial value the first time, and returns the value with a setter.

    ```{javascript}
    const hooks = [];
    let hookIndex = 0;
    let renders = 0;

    function _performRender() {
      hookIndex = 0;
      renders = renders + 1;
    }

    function useState(initialVal) {
      // TODO: take the current index, move hookIndex on by one,
      // store initialVal in the slot if the slot is still undefined,
      // and return [current value, setState]
    }

    _performRender();

    const [count, setCount] = useState(5);

    // Captured before the setter runs, because a re-render resets the counter.
    const indexAfterFirstHook = hookIndex;

    setCount(6);

    console.log(count, hooks[0], indexAfterFirstHook, renders);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const hooks = [];
    let hookIndex = 0;
    let renders = 0;

    function _performRender() {
      hookIndex = 0;
      renders = renders + 1;
    }

    function useState(initialVal) {
      const idx = hookIndex;

      hookIndex = hookIndex + 1;

      if (hooks[idx] === undefined) {
        hooks[idx] = initialVal;
      }

      function setState(newVal) {
        if (hooks[idx] !== newVal) {
          hooks[idx] = newVal;

          _performRender();
        }
      }

      return [hooks[idx], setState];
    }

    _performRender();

    const [count, setCount] = useState(5);

    // Captured before the setter runs, because a re-render resets the counter.
    const indexAfterFirstHook = hookIndex;

    setCount(6);

    console.log(count, hooks[0], indexAfterFirstHook, renders);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(count).should('equal', 5).run('The first render should see the initial value, `5`', 'Returned the initial value');
    wrap(hooks[0]).should('equal', 6).run('Calling the setter should write the new value into the hook slot', 'The setter updated the slot');
    wrap(indexAfterFirstHook).should('equal', 1).run('Each `useState` call should move `hookIndex` on by one, so it claims the next slot', 'Advanced the hook index');
    wrap(renders).should('equal', 2).run('Changing the value should trigger a render, so `_performRender` runs a second time', 'Triggered a re-render');
    ```

2. Add the two refinements from the reading: a setter that ignores an unchanged value, and one that accepts an updater function.

    ```{javascript}
    const hooks = [10];
    let renderCount = 0;

    function _performRender() {
      renderCount = renderCount + 1;
    }

    function setState(newVal) {
      // TODO: if newVal is a function, call it with the current value hooks[0]
      // to get the real new value. Then, only if the value actually changed,
      // store it and call _performRender().
    }

    setState(10);                    // same value: nothing should happen
    setState(11);                    // changed
    setState(function (v) { return v + 1; });   // updater form

    console.log(hooks[0], renderCount);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const hooks = [10];
    let renderCount = 0;

    function _performRender() {
      renderCount = renderCount + 1;
    }

    function setState(newVal) {
      if (typeof newVal === 'function') {
        newVal = newVal(hooks[0]);
      }

      if (hooks[0] !== newVal) {
        hooks[0] = newVal;

        _performRender();
      }
    }

    setState(10);
    setState(11);
    setState(function (v) { return v + 1; });

    console.log(hooks[0], renderCount);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(hooks[0]).should('equal', 12).run('After 10, then 11, then an updater adding one, the value should be `12`', 'Handled both forms');
    wrap(renderCount).should('equal', 2).run('Setting the value it already had should render nothing, so only two renders should happen', 'Skipped the no-op update');
    ```

3. Why hook order matters

    ```{mcq}
    What identifies which state variable a `useState` call is talking about?
    correct: Correct! Its position in the hooks array, tracked by a counter that resets each render. Skipping a call inside an `if` shifts every later hook into the wrong slot, and a component starts reading another variable's value.
    wrong: Look at what `useState` does with `hookIndex` before it does anything else. Nothing about the variable's name is ever recorded.
    [x] Its position in the hooks array, so a skipped call would shift all the later ones
    [ ] The name of the variable it was assigned to
    [ ] The component's name plus the initial value
    [ ] Nothing; React matches them by comparing values
    ```

4. The value right after the setter

    ```{mcq}
    You call `setCount(count + 1)` and then log `count` on the next line. Why is it unchanged?
    correct: Correct! The setter writes to the hooks array and schedules a render for later. `count` is a value captured when this render produced the handler, and only a new render produces a new one.
    wrong: Look at what `setState` does after storing the value, and when the component function next runs. Revisit the `setTimeout` note in the walkthrough.
    [x] The setter schedules a re-render for later, and `count` belongs to the render that created the handler
    [ ] The setter failed, because the value did not change
    [ ] `console.log` reads a cached copy of the variable
    [ ] State updates only take effect after a full page reload
    ```

